-### Parameters and Typical Values
-*   **k (K-Buffer size):** In the cited implementation, $k=3$ [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Framebuffer Resolution:** 512x512 pixels in experimental simulations [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Factor:** 2.1 to 3.6 times the Z-buffer size for CAD models; supports average depth complexity of 10 fragments per pixel with 5x framebuffer storage [S3, unofficial].
-
-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Holes/Concavities:** Properly handling holes requires dynamic tagging of vertices as boundary/internal and k-buffer entries as internal/external [S2, unofficial]. Ray segments with both vertices on the boundary should have zero color/opacity [S2, unofficial]. However, the logic to update these tags dynamically exceeds current hardware instruction limits in the cited implementation [S2, unofficial]. Consequently, the implementation uses only a single tag for initialization and does not handle holes properly [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Hardware Status:** The original R-buffer hardware does not exist; implementations are software simulations or GPU shader approximations [S1].
-*   **Performance:** The software version does more work than incremental sorting algorithms (like Wilhelms et al.) because sorts are repeated from scratch independently at each pixel [S1].
-*   **Precision:** View-space depth $d$ must be accurately calculated in the vertex program and passed to the fragment stage to ensure correct sorting [S2, unofficial].
+### Parameters and Edge Cases
+*   **k (Buffer Size):** While the R-Buffer is global, the compositing logic often references a "k-buffer" concept where $k$ entries are stored per pixel in the software simulation. In the hardware R-Buffer, $k$ is effectively dynamic and limited by the total R-Buffer size [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Holes/Concavities:** Handling holes in the data requires tagging vertices as boundary/internal and k-buffer entries as internal/external. Ray segments with both vertices on the boundary are assigned zero color/opacity [S2, unofficial]. This classification must be dynamically updated, which exceeds current hardware instruction limits in some implementations, leading to improper hole handling in early versions [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Screen Position Storage:** To enable per-pixel operations in a global buffer, every fragment must store its original screen position $(X, Y)$ [S5, unofficial]. This increases fragment size compared to per-pixel buffers [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Early Ray Termination:** Front-to-back compositing with an adaptive opacity threshold can terminate the sorting process early, limiting the number of layers to be sorted [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Run time complexity depends on the average depth complexity squared ($O(D^2)$) [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is proportional to the projected area of all faces in the mesh, unlike methods proportional only to the boundary [S1]. The R-buffer amortizes this over the screen, requiring 2.1 to 3.6 times the Z-buffer memory for detailed models [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:**
-    *   **Phoenix Dataset:** 1022k pixels, average depth complexity 27.6, 66 passes, tot/Z bandwidth ratio 17.4 [S3, unofficial].
-    *   **Head Full Dataset:** 9287k pixels, average depth complexity 201.6, 475 passes, tot/Z bandwidth ratio 135.1 [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:**
-    *   Handles arbitrary depth complexity without fixed limits on transparent layers [S3, unofficial].
-    *   Single geometry pass required; no repeated rasterization of primitives [S3, unofficial].
-    *   Amortizes storage costs across the entire screen, efficient for irregular grids with varying fragment counts [S3, unofficial].
-    *   Unassociated color storage with associated compositing reduces triangle artifacts at model edges [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:**
-    *   High memory overhead compared to Z-buffering [S3, unofficial].
-    *   Performance degrades significantly with high depth complexity due to quadratic pass count [S3, unofficial].
-    *   Current hardware implementations struggle with dynamic hole handling (concavities) [S2, unofficial].
-    *   Not suitable for volume rendering where the number of fragments is proportional to the entire mesh surface area rather than just the boundary [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The run time complexity depends on the square of the average depth complexity ($O(D^2)$) [S4, unofficial]. The number of recirculating passes is related to the worst-case depth complexity of any pixel [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is proportional to the projected area of all faces in the mesh, unlike methods proportional only to the mesh boundary [S1]. Experiments show required memory to be 2.1 to 3.6 times higher than a traditional Z-buffer for detailed CAD models [S4, unofficial]. A factor of 5x frame buffer storage supports an average depth complexity of 10 fragments per pixel [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** On the Phoenix dataset (1022k pixels, depth complexity 27.6), the R-buffer required 66 passes and a total-to-Z bandwidth ratio of 17.4 [S4, unofficial]. On the Head Full dataset (9287k pixels, depth complexity 201.6), it required 475 passes and a ratio of 135.1 [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Single pass geometry rasterization; amortized storage cost over the entire screen; handles arbitrary depth complexity without fixed per-pixel limits [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** High memory overhead for high-depth-complexity scenes; run time increases quadratically with depth complexity; hardware has not been implemented [S1]. Storing screen coordinates in every fragment is a drawback compared to linked-list approaches [S5, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The R-Buffer was proposed by Wittenbrink as a hardware component for rendering tetrahedral primitives [S1]. It is similar in spirit to Carpenter’s software A-buffer but implements the sorting in hardware to avoid the inefficiencies of Z-buffering (which is a read-modify-write operation and does not perform an actual sort) [S3, unofficial]. The concept was further explored in the context of GPU implementations via the "k-buffer" technique by Callahan et al., which uses Multiple Render Targets to simulate the recirculating buffer behavior [S2, unofficial]. It is primarily associated with unstructured tetrahedral volume rendering and order-independent transparency [S3, unofficial].
+The R-Buffer was proposed by Wittenbrink [S1] as a hardware architecture for rendering tetrahedral primitives, implementing a method similar to Mammen's software algorithm [S1]. It was further developed and simulated by King et al. [S4, unofficial] for unstructured tetrahedral volume rendering. The concept is a pointerless A-buffer hardware architecture [S3]. It was designed to replace per-primitive sorting with per-pixel sorting to handle high depth complexity efficiently in hardware [S4, unofficial].
 
